{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1481924-teamwork-rolls","id":1481924,"title":"Teamwork rolls","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-05-25T08:10:06-07:00","id":1481924,"title":"Teamwork rolls","content":"Just realized that I've messed up.<br /> <br /> I guess I have poor reading comprehension and so I was using for my group a teamwork roll that I have no idea where I got it from (maybe SR5?).<br /> I have one person be the point for the action, the players decide based on who makes the most sense to take the lead, whether that be due to skill, or initiative or access. Then all the assistants roll their relevant pools at their relevant difficulties. They add all their successes on those rolls as bonus dice to the point-persons pool which is rolled last.<br /> <br /> This worked fine in V20, but as I'm trying to run W20 and things like pack tactics are coming up I double checked the rules and realized how different they are. In all honesty, I don't like the base teamwork rules, I think they apply best to a circumstance like a racecar pit crew, where everyone has a job, knows how to do it, and everyone who is assisting can focus totally on their own part of the job and already know how to support any of their partners.<br /> <br /> I think my, I guess houserule, better accommodates the two idiots staring at an engine trying to figure out what 'top dead center' means while some other idiot reads a forumn post from the computer in the other room. <br /> <br /> But it doesn't make any sense in things like dogpile and fur gnarl. In no small part because I can't easily slot in who is the point person and who is supporting. And like, do high initiative people slow down to lower init packmates or do low init packmates get dragged onto the pack tactic initiators tempo? If it's the former, then I feel like any high initiative opponent just gets the hell out of the way of what they can see coming, if it's the latter then it seems like there's no reason not to use a pack tactic every time you outspeed your opponent.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27296-devidramoth","name":"Devidramoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27296_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"54"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-29T21:05:18-07:00","id":1482337,"title":"","content":"You didn't got the Team Work rules wrong.<br /> <br /> Pack Tactics isn't Team Work in rules terms.<br /> <br /> A Pack Tactic is exactly that, a tactic. It is a structured arrangement that the Pack will devise, test and then start training until everyone can do their part seamlessly in a concerted effort to achieve a greater goal. So when combat starts someone just shouts &quot;Plan XYZ&quot; and everyone knows exactly what to do and what the others are going to do.<br /> <br /> So... yeah, it is actually exactly like a racecar pitcrew and nothing like two idiots looking at an engine, because that's the idea.<br /> <br /> When you want regular team work, go with your rule, I think it is the one you uses already. In a combat you could have this kind of Team Work when several characters try to grapple someone, or other situations similar to D&amp;D's &quot;Aid Another&quot; rule.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}